1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,736
The live Week 15 Moodle activity is labelled Course 9A: MCI, but its
Preview SCORM opens a different internal title: Transport Logistics
and Flight Physiology for Primary Care Paramedics. The internal body
contains five teaching lessons and a glossary.

2
00:00:17,736 --> 00:00:35,280
This course fills that verified Week 15 gap. It does not replace the
separate MCI/START track, and it does not reproduce embedded knowledge
checks. Current BCEHS helicopter procedures govern local field safety
where generic course statements conflict.

3
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:50,976
A moving ambulance, boat or aircraft changes what the crew can reach,
see, hear and do. Transport planning therefore includes what care is
feasible in motion, what must be addressed first, and how help will be
summoned if the patient worsens.

4
00:00:50,976 --> 00:01:05,424
Use the whole pathway: scene-to-vehicle, vehicle-to-destination, then
handover. Reassess after major movement and whenever the patient,
route, weather, resources or landing site changes.

5
00:01:05,424 --> 00:01:24,576
Air transport can shorten a segment of the journey or reach a remote
area, but the relevant comparison is time to appropriate definitive
care—not flight time alone. Include activation, aircraft availability,
weather, landing, patient transfer and onward ground travel.

6
00:01:24,576 --> 00:01:44,160
Use the school lesson’s four-T memory aid—time, terrain/traffic,
treatment capability and team availability—to organize the discussion.
It is a study aid, not an authorization rule; coordinate with
dispatch, clinical oversight, the receiving service and flight crew as
required.

7
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:58,728
Ask what time is actually saved after the response and transfer
sequence is counted. A helicopter may not be the quickest option for a
nearby hospital, an unsafe site or a patient whose immediate needs
cannot be met during flight.

8
00:01:58,728 --> 00:02:10,752
Do not suspend immediate care merely to await a preferred mode.
Reassess when the patient or weather changes and communicate the
updated plan through the proper operational and clinical channels.

9
00:02:10,752 --> 00:02:25,152
Before loading, identify which assessments and interventions may be
needed en route. Place and secure equipment so that it does not snag,
disconnect or block access; follow the device instructions and service
procedure.

10
00:02:25,152 --> 00:02:42,144
Protect the patient from cold, heat, unnecessary exposure and
avoidable movement. In rotary-wing operations, loose blankets,
clothing, cables and bags can be lifted by downwash, so secure all
items before the aircraft approaches.

11
00:02:42,144 --> 00:02:54,912
Patient packaging does not prove stability. Continue the appropriate
monitoring, compare trends, and reassess after stretcher loading,
aircraft transfer, bumps or a clinical change.

12
00:02:54,912 --> 00:03:08,808
If an intervention cannot be done safely while moving, coordinate
whether the vehicle should stop or the transport plan should change.
Communicate early; do not let the desire to arrive quickly hide a
deteriorating patient.

13
00:03:08,808 --> 00:03:22,056
An ambulance is heavier and often taller than a passenger car. Its
braking, acceleration and cornering response differ, especially when
loaded or on wet, uneven or icy roads.

14
00:03:22,056 --> 00:03:35,256
Smooth control protects the patient, the working partner and
equipment. Lights and sirens do not remove road hazards or justify a
speed that is unsafe for visibility, traction or traffic.

15
00:03:35,256 --> 00:03:48,360
The source lesson emphasizes vehicle readiness, road weather and
defensive driving. Make the pre-trip check a real, locally defined
checklist rather than an invented universal maintenance procedure.

16
00:03:48,360 --> 00:04:04,512
A stable patient does not make an unsafe road safe. If visibility,
traction, vehicle behaviour or another hazard becomes unacceptable,
prioritize safe control, communicate the delay or stop and revisit the
route with the appropriate team.

17
00:04:04,512 --> 00:04:19,848
The BCEHS Spring 2026 procedure describes a Landing Zone Manager role
for selection, hazard communication, readiness and perimeter control.
The manager should not be simultaneously occupied with scene
operations or patient care.

18
00:04:19,848 --> 00:04:33,144
Coordinate selection with Incident Command and relevant responders.
The pilot-in-command has final authority over whether the site and
approach are suitable; the flight crew may choose another location or
decline to land.

19
00:04:33,144 --> 00:04:53,472
The current public BCEHS Spring 2026 briefing defines the LZ as a
safety zone plus the touchdown point. It gives a safety-zone guide of
at least 40 paces by 40 paces, approximately 40 metres by 40 metres,
and a preferred separation of at least 50 paces from the patient or
accident area.

20
00:04:53,472 --> 00:05:12,480
The Moodle lesson also displays a generic 100-by-100-foot figure, then
a different BCEHS dimension. Do not blend these into one rule: for
BCEHS operations use its current 40-pace procedure and follow the
responding aircraft’s requirements. The pilot retains final authority.

21
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:28,488
Walk and scan the whole safety zone when feasible. The BCEHS procedure
recommends at least 50 metres between the LZ edge and power lines; if
a site is closer, the hazard must be briefed to the pilot, and an
alternative location may be safer.

22
00:05:28,488 --> 00:05:42,072
Thin wires can be difficult to see from the air, particularly at
night. Identify obstacles by direction and landmark, report them
clearly, and never assume a light, cone or marker makes a wire safe.

23
00:05:42,072 --> 00:05:56,064
A landing zone is more than an empty patch of ground. The team needs a
clear perimeter, a patient-transfer route and a shared understanding
of who controls access, who communicates and what happens if
conditions change.

24
00:05:56,064 --> 00:06:08,400
Do not use people as markers around the landing perimeter. People may
control access from a safe position, while visible markers are used
only as approved by the current procedure and flight crew.

25
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:23,136
Use the authorized channel and the local agency’s call format. Report
the status plainly: ready, not ready, or how many minutes are needed.
Give the LZ location and its relationship to the scene or a known
landmark.

26
00:06:23,136 --> 00:06:38,688
Include estimated wind direction and speed, visibility and every
relevant obstacle. Update the report when conditions change. Do not
direct the aircraft’s approach path; transmit observations and let the
pilot decide.

27
00:06:38,688 --> 00:06:52,704
Night-vision equipment does not make darkness equivalent to daylight.
Weather, dust, snow, rain, smoke, glare and narrow obstacles can
reduce visibility even when crews use night-vision goggles.

28
00:06:52,704 --> 00:07:08,616
BCEHS notes that some LED beacons may be invisible to night-vision
goggles and that crews may request strobes be turned off. Use only
approved lighting, never improvise aircraft-directed illumination, and
follow the crew’s current instructions.

29
00:07:08,616 --> 00:07:38,520
On final approach, the landing-zone manager clears outside the safety
zone and watches for hazards, keeping radio traffic safety-critical.
If a hazard appears, transmit: abort, abort, abort. The BCEHS visual
alternative is to extend both arms overhead and wave them in a
crossing motion. If in doubt, call the abort; remain in communication
and explain the hazard. The pilot-in-command retains the landing
decision.

30
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:53,496
Do not clear an aircraft into an LZ that is not ready. If the aircraft
overshoots or aborts, remain in communication and follow the agreed
next step; do not restart the approach until the hazard and
authorization are resolved.

31
00:07:53,496 --> 00:08:12,336
Transport Canada advises following crew instructions and avoiding
rotor hazards. The current BCEHS Spring 2026 LZ procedure is more
specific for local scene operations: do not approach until the rotors
have completely stopped and a pilot gets out and waves responders
forward.

32
00:08:12,336 --> 00:08:28,440
The Moodle lesson’s generic “front or side while crouched” wording
conflicts with that current BCEHS rule. Do not use it to approach an
active aircraft. Aircraft type and operator procedures vary; the
flight crew controls the authorized route.

33
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:47,256
Identify yourself as the primary ground caregiver and give the flight
paramedic a short, structured report: what happened, what you found,
what changed, what you did and the current vital-sign trend. Include
identification or known allergies when available and appropriate.

34
00:08:47,256 --> 00:09:06,120
Follow the flight paramedic and pilot directions for loading and
transfer. BCEHS says not to load equipment or operate aircraft doors
unless asked. Keep the site clear after takeoff; its 2026 briefing
says maintain the LZ for about five minutes in case the aircraft must
return.

35
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:19,320
During ascent, lower ambient pressure can expand a closed gas
collection. The Week-15 school lesson emphasizes that this can matter
for pneumothorax, other trapped gas and air-containing equipment.

36
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:34,848
The size of the effect depends on actual cabin altitude,
pressurization, flight profile and the patient or device. Do not
assume every helicopter flight produces the same pressure change or
make an intervention solely from the gas-law principle.

37
00:09:34,848 --> 00:09:48,576
Oxygen remains about 21 percent of dry air, but the total atmospheric
pressure decreases with altitude. The result is a lower oxygen partial
pressure, which can worsen hypoxia in a vulnerable patient.

38
00:09:48,576 --> 00:10:05,352
Actual exposure varies with the aircraft, cabin pressure, route and
operating altitude. Follow the current clinical protocol and flight-
team plan for oxygen, monitoring and escalation; there is no universal
altitude or saturation rule in this lesson.

39
00:10:05,352 --> 00:10:17,880
A helicopter cabin can be noisy, vibratory and constrained. The course
teaches these as operational stressors, not as proof that a particular
measurement is always impossible.

40
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:33,624
Before flight, confirm with the flight crew which equipment is
available, where it can be secured and how communication will work. Do
not rely on a monitor reading without considering motion, signal
quality, device limits and the patient’s full presentation.

41
00:10:33,624 --> 00:10:47,208
For a patient at risk from gas expansion or hypoxia, identify the
concern before flight, communicate it to the flight crew and agree on
monitoring, equipment and escalation under the approved protocol.

42
00:10:47,208 --> 00:11:03,000
The Moodle lesson mentions changing the volume of air-filled devices.
This track does not direct you to deflate an endotracheal cuff or
alter any device. Use the device instructions, scope, medical
direction and flight-provider procedure.

43
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:20,136
In this original case, do not choose “air” merely because it is
shorter on a map. Compare the whole time to appropriate care, the
patient’s changing condition, weather, available crew, route and
whether a secure landing and transfer can actually be completed.

44
00:11:20,136 --> 00:11:36,960
If wind or rain makes the LZ unsafe, report the hazard and wait for a
safe plan. Continue appropriate care while coordinating; once transfer
is authorized, reassess after each major movement and hand over the
patient’s status and trend.

45
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:49,896
If a number or approach rule differs between a generic module and the
current BCEHS briefing, stop and resolve the discrepancy before
practice. Do not blend sources into a third, invented procedure.

46
00:11:49,896 --> 00:12:06,792
This narrated course prepares your reasoning; it does not certify
driving, LZ management, aircraft approach, flight procedures or
medical competency. Hands-on actions require the school instructor,
current operator procedures and supervised practice.
